menu "LCTrace kernel tracing"

config KFEAT_LCTRACE
    bool "LCTrace low-overhead kernel tracing"
    default n
    help
      Record kernel events into a fixed-size, lock-free, per-CPU ring buffer
      so that the recent past can be recovered after a crash. Emitting a
      record costs one uncontended atomic increment, one counter read and a
      few relaxed stores; nothing allocates and nothing is formatted until a
      snapshot is read.

      Snapshots are rendered by reading /proc/lctrace, and the panic handler
      dumps every ring to the console after the backtrace.

      This is a debug facility and is unrelated to the ftrace-compatible
      tracepoint subsystem selected by KFEAT_TRACEPOINT. Leave it disabled
      unless a specific investigation needs it: the probes sit on the
      scheduler, IRQ and syscall paths, and the rings consume
      NR_CPUS * LCTRACE_ENTRIES_PER_CPU * 64 bytes of .bss.

config LCTRACE_ENTRIES_PER_CPU
    usize "LCTrace records retained per CPU"
    depends on KFEAT_LCTRACE
    range 64 8192
    default 512
    help
      Number of records each CPU retains before overwriting the oldest.

      Must be a power of two; the kernel fails to build otherwise, because
      the ring index is computed with a mask rather than a division.

      Each record occupies one 64-byte cache line, so the default of 512
      costs 32 KiB of .bss per CPU. The buffer lives in .bss and therefore
      does not enlarge the kernel image on disk.

endmenu
